WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Alina Igorevna Kiryukhina presents her work, titled “Dedication to Father.” Supervisor: Khamit Vladimirovich Sobkuev; reviewer: Polina Vladimirovna Nikitina.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Alina Igorevna Kiryukhina, a citizen of the Russian Federation, was born in the village of Tyubuk in the Chelyabinsk Region. She graduated from the Sverdlovsk Art College in Yekaterinburg in 2016 and entered the Academy the same year.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Alina Igorevna completed all examinations and assessments required by the curriculum across six years of the Faculty of Painting.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Hello everyone. This series of works is my memories of childhood — a kind of collective image of the Urals and of my family.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:04.000
My mother and I lived in a military settlement. Among the village stood a couple of Soviet panel apartment blocks; we lived in one of them.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:14.000
For almost all of my childhood my father was not with us — he served in Siberia. He always came to us or left us by train; there was no other transport.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.000
My mother and I constantly heard the clatter of wheels; a railway station was not far away.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:27.000
As a child it seemed to me that a train was something endless — as if it were traveling across Russia and could never reach the end.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:40.000
All of Russia also felt endless, and everywhere it should look the same: panel blocks, old wooden houses, rust, the railway, wells.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Everywhere there should be a forest nearby, and somewhere there a person who almost always wears a military uniform.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:02:02.000
In my works, through the feeling of waiting, nostalgia, a sense of being left behind, through all this tender sadness, I wanted to convey hope for the light —

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:10.000
and that within it there is an immense love for my father, for my family, and for my homeland. Thank you.
